A||||6||Master_Sushi||Cleaned up big spill...||||14||
Z||000000||Master_Sushi||09-20-2000||12:45 PM||||I ended up pulling both seats yesterday and cleaning the interior. I used soapy water and a shop vac. The result was Great, my gray seats came out so clean they look white. And my carpeting looks brand new. I would recommend that anyone who hasen't done this allready to do so. I also found a 20$ bill under the drivers seat(and allot of other "odd" stuff), so that made my day  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> . I also did a quick oil change, the iron duke sure has a strange oil filter. So all in all my cars running really well now. <P>Thanks for the space  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000001||StuGood||09-22-2000||12:55 PM||||I've known for a long time that Fieros are great cars, because they reward you if you treat them nicely.  First time I ever heard of one leaving a big tip, like that, though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000002||Fformula88||09-22-2000||04:49 PM||||I'm getting my tools out now!  I want a big tip from my Fiero too!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>BTW, how hard was it to remove the seats.  It looks like it would be difficult to get to the rear bolts.  I need to get under the carpeting on my dads 87 coupe to fix a hole in the floorboards, and would like to remove the seat to do it.  However, I am afraid that its going to be a massive job to get those seats out.  <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/Motorcity/Boulevard/9278/fierosig1.jpg">  <BR>1988 Fiero Formula||||reg||1
Z||000003||87ECO5||09-22-2000||04:53 PM||||It's way too easy, Fformula88.  4 bolts (13mm?) - slide the seat back, take out the front two.  Slide the seat forward, take out the back two.<P><B>CAUTION!!!!!</B>  Be careful when removing the seats from the passenger compartment!  I wasn't watching what I was doing and ended up putting a nice, big scratch on the door panel with one of the rails!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 09-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||09-23-2000||03:11 PM||||13mm is right.  And to make it easier to put the seats back in, don't lift the seat off the studs until all 4 nuts are out.  Fold the seat forward using the recliner lever, give the seat a bear hug and carefully lift it out.<P>While seats are out, try not to actuate  the lever in front (the one that allows you to slide the seat).  Otherwise  the runners can get out of synch - which makes it harder to reinstall.  Not the end of the world - just a little more work!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Master_Sushi||09-23-2000||04:36 PM||||Yes, it is very easy to do, and well worth it. Took me about 3 mins to take each seat out. And about 2 hours to do all the carpet. Oddly the bolts and runners on my seats were rusty also. So I ended up sanding and spray painting them. But it all looks good as new now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.||||reg||14
Z||000006||deceler8||09-25-2000||12:14 PM||||Glad it worked as well for you.<P>My Fiero "tip" was in the form of about $5 in change under the driver's seat. <P>||||reg||1
